{"title":"Ishika's Garden Discovery","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Why Leaves are Green","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was helping her grandmother water the plants on their apartment balcony one sunny morning. She noticed the bright green leaves on the neem tree and wondered out loud, 'Dadi, why are all these leaves so beautifully green?'"},{"label":"problem","text":"Her grandmother smiled and said, 'Come, let me show you something special.' She held a leaf up to the sunlight. 'Look closely—do you see how the sun's light passes through it? There is something inside that leaf making it green, and that same thing helps the plant make its own food.'"},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"Dadi explained, 'Inside every green leaf is a special substance called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll is green in colour, and it catches the sunlight. When sunlight falls on the chlorophyll, the leaf uses that light energy, along with water from the soil and air, to make food for the plant. This is called photosynthesis. That is why leaves must be green—they need chlorophyll to do this important work and stay alive.'"},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika looked at the neem leaves again with new eyes. 'So the green colour is like the leaf's special power to eat sunlight!' she said happily. From that day, every time she watered the plants, she remembered that those green leaves were busy making food and keeping the plant strong, and she felt proud to help them grow."}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"If you found 3 different plants in your home or garden, and each one had green leaves for the same reason—to catch sunlight and make food through photosynthesis—how many plants in total would be using chlorophyll to stay alive?","answer":"3 plants, all using chlorophyll to catch sunlight and make food through photosynthesis."},"parentPrompt":"Look together at a green leaf at home or outside. Ask: Can you see how the green colour helps it catch light?"}
